{
  "image_name": "0d4e55c15667ae6802f1fd118983edc4.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how the shelterin complex is organized on telomeric DNA and how its subunits connect double-stranded and single-stranded chromosome ends. At the top left, the complex is shown assembled at the telomeric dsDNA–ssDNA junction, with TRF1 and TRF2 bound to double-stranded telomeric repeats, POT1 bound to the single-stranded overhang, and TIN2, TPP1, and RAP1 acting as connecting components.\n\nThe central diagrams break the proteins into domains and interaction regions. TRF1 and TRF2 each contain a TRFH dimerization module and a Myb DNA-binding domain for telomeric dsDNA. POT1 has multiple OB-fold regions that mediate telomeric ssDNA binding, while TPP1 contains an OB domain with a TEL patch linked to POT1 and TIN2. TIN2 appears to bridge the dsDNA-binding factors to the POT1–TPP1 unit, and RAP1 associates with TRF2.\n\nThe lower schematic integrates these interactions into a broader model of telomere architecture. TRF1 and TRF2 homodimers bind the duplex region, TIN2 links them to POT1–TPP1 at the single-stranded end, and this arrangement supports two key functions: end protection by POT1 and telomerase recruitment through the TPP1 OB/TEL patch region.",
